{"schemaVersion":1,"key":"fundamentals/multi-agent","track":"engineers","lang":"en","dir":"ltr","title":"Multi-Agent Systems","objectives":{"lang":"en","items":["Explain what kind of complexity pushes you toward multiple agents","Compare the supervisor and network architectures","Name the two hard problems of multi-agent systems: coordination and context transfer","Explain the difference between shared and isolated context","Recognise when a single agent is still the right answer"]},"prerequisites":[{"key":"fundamentals/agents","number":6,"title":"AI Agents","drawn":true,"track":"engineers","lang":"en"}],"previous":{"key":"fundamentals/agents","number":6,"title":"AI Agents","drawn":true,"track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"next":{"key":"fundamentals/observability","number":8,"title":"Observability","drawn":true,"track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"toc":[{"id":"why-we-might-need-multi-agents","text":"Why We Might Need Multi-agents","depth":2},{"id":"basic-multi-agent-architectures","text":"Basic Multi-agent Architectures","depth":2},{"id":"multi-agent-drawbacks","text":"Multi-agent Drawbacks","depth":2},{"id":"shared-context-or-isolated-context","text":"Shared Context, or Isolated Context","depth":3},{"id":"advanced-multi-agent-architectures","text":"Advanced Multi-agent Architectures","depth":2},{"id":"building-a-multi-agent","text":"Building a Multi-agent","depth":2},{"id":"when-a-single-agent-might-be-better","text":"When a Single Agent Might be Better","depth":2},{"id":"summary","text":"Summary","depth":2},{"id":"references","text":"References","depth":2},{"id":"-articles","text":"📄 Articles","depth":3},{"id":"-modules","text":"🧭 Modules","depth":3}],"body":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/agents","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"AI Agents"}]},{"type":"text","text":" ended with one agent: one loop, one system prompt, one set of tools. This module is about what happens when you use several agents, and what that costs you."}],"lead":true},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"why-we-might-need-multi-agents","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Why We Might Need Multi-agents"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Take a real request: "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"\"How many rows are in my database tables? Show them in a bar chart.\""}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"That is two different jobs. First you write and run SQL, then you draw a chart. One agent can do both, but you pay a price for it. The system prompt now has to explain SQL "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"and"}]},{"type":"text","text":" charting, and the tool list gets longer. Every call also carries instructions that have nothing to do with the current step. So the model has more ways to pick the wrong tool, and more room to confuse the two jobs."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"If you split the work, each agent gets a short prompt, a small tool list and one job:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"a "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"SQL agent"}]},{"type":"text","text":" that writes and runs queries"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"a "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"visualisation agent"}]},{"type":"text","text":" that turns results into charts"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Each one is just an agent from "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/agents","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"AI Agents"}]},{"type":"text","text":". Nothing new has been invented. You have only gone from one loop to several loops."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"basic-multi-agent-architectures","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Basic Multi-agent Architectures"}]},{"type":"image","number":"7.1","title":"Single agent, network and supervisor architectures","note":"Left: one agent with its tools. This is where AI Agents ended. Middle: a network, where every agent can talk to every other agent. Right: a supervisor. It receives the request and delegates the work to workers, and the workers only talk to the supervisor.","src":"course-images/fundamentals/multiagents.png","alt":"Single agent, network and supervisor architectures","width":4382,"height":1499},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Supervisor."}]},{"type":"text","text":" One agent receives the request. Then, in this order, it:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"decides which worker should handle the request"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"passes the work to that worker"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"collects what comes back"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"answers the user"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Workers talk to the supervisor and to no one else. Our SQL and chart example fits this shape exactly. The supervisor sends the question to the SQL agent and takes the rows. Then it hands the rows to the visualisation agent and returns the chart."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"You will also see this called manager-worker or orchestrator-worker. It is the same shape."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Network, sometimes called a swarm."}]},{"type":"text","text":" There is no boss. Every agent can hand work to any other agent. The agents decide among themselves who does what next. This is more flexible. It is also much harder to predict, because there is no single place where you can see what the system decided."}]},{"type":"diagram","number":"7.2","title":"Basic Multi-agent Architectures","svg":{"light":"api/v1/diagrams/665ea41370f9f7c930f1f7a8207fa7e0.svg","dark":"api/v1/diagrams/8b6be31b8d68af85ccec67f6c7962ade.svg"}},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Start with a supervisor."}]},{"type":"text","text":" It is easier to debug, because every decision passes through one place. Also, most problems that look like they need a swarm do not need one."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"multi-agent-drawbacks","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Multi-agent Drawbacks"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"This is the part to take seriously, because both problems come "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"from"}]},{"type":"text","text":" splitting the work. A single agent has neither."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Coordination."}]},{"type":"text","text":" Who does what, and in what order? How does anyone know that a step is finished? With one agent, the loop answered these questions. With several agents, something has to decide, and that decision can be wrong. Two agents can do the same work. An agent can wait for something that never arrives. A supervisor can keep delegating and never stop. More agents means more ways to deadlock, which is when agents wait for each other forever. It also means more ways to loop without end."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Context transfer."}]},{"type":"text","text":" Each agent has its own context: its own message stack. So when the SQL agent finishes, what exactly does the visualisation agent get? Does it get only the rows? Does it also get the original question? Does it get the SQL that produced the rows? If you hand over too little, the second agent has to work without information that it needs. If you hand over everything, you pay for a huge context. You also bring back the confusion that you split the agents to avoid."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Getting this wrong is the usual reason why a multi-agent system performs "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"worse"}]},{"type":"text","text":" than the single agent it replaced."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"shared-context-or-isolated-context","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Shared Context, or Isolated Context"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"That leads to a choice that sits under every multi-agent design. Do the agents "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"share"}]},{"type":"text","text":" one context, or does each agent work in its own "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"isolated"}]},{"type":"text","text":" context?"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Shared:"}]},{"type":"text","text":" everyone sees everything. Nothing gets lost in the handover, but the context grows fast."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Isolated:"}]},{"type":"text","text":" each agent sees only what it was handed. This is cheap and focused. But whatever you forgot to pass along is simply gone."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Production systems use both. This choice decides almost everything else about the design. The topic is deep enough to deserve its own discussion, so we come back to it in "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"expert/advanced-multiagent","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Advanced Multi-Agent"}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"advanced-multi-agent-architectures","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Advanced Multi-agent Architectures"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"We name these here so that the words are familiar. We cover all of them later:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Hierarchical:"}]},{"type":"text","text":" a top supervisor manages other supervisors, and each of them manages its own workers. You use this when one layer is not enough."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Agent-as-a-tool:"}]},{"type":"text","text":" one agent is given to another agent as if it were a plain tool. This fits directly into the mechanism from "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/tools","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Tool Calling"}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Subagents:"}]},{"type":"text","text":" an agent starts short-lived helpers, and each helper has its own isolated context. The agent then keeps only their results."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"building-a-multi-agent","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Building a Multi-agent"}]},{"type":"code","language":"python","lines":[[{"text":"from","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" smolagents "},{"text":"import","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" CodeAgent, tool, InferenceClientModel"}],[],[{"text":"@tool","role":"function"}],[{"text":"def","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" sql_query","role":"function"},{"text":"(query: str) -> list:"}],[{"text":"    \"\"\"Run a SQL query against the application database and return the rows.\"\"\"","role":"string"}],[{"text":"    return","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" db."},{"text":"execute(query)","role":"function"},{"text":"."},{"text":"fetchall()","role":"function"}],[],[{"text":"@tool","role":"function"}],[{"text":"def","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" visualise","role":"function"},{"text":"(data: list) -> str:"}],[{"text":"    \"\"\"Draw a bar chart from rows and return the path to the image.\"\"\"","role":"string"}],[{"text":"    return","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" chart_from(data)","role":"function"}],[],[{"text":"sql_agent "},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" CodeAgent(tools","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"[sql_query], model","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"InferenceClientModel(), name","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"\"sql\"","role":"string"},{"text":",","role":"function"}],[{"text":"                      description","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"\"Answers questions by querying the database.\"","role":"string"},{"text":")","role":"function"}],[{"text":"viz_agent "},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" CodeAgent(tools","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"[visualise], model","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"InferenceClientModel(), name","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"\"viz\"","role":"string"},{"text":",","role":"function"}],[{"text":"                      description","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"\"Draws a chart from rows of data.\"","role":"string"},{"text":")","role":"function"}],[],[{"text":"supervisor "},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" CodeAgent(","role":"function"}],[{"text":"    tools","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"[],","role":"function"}],[{"text":"    model","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"InferenceClientModel(),","role":"function"}],[{"text":"    managed_agents","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"[sql_agent, viz_agent],   ","role":"function"},{"text":"# the workers it can delegate to","role":"comment"}],[{"text":")","role":"function"}],[],[{"text":"supervisor."},{"text":"run(","role":"function"},{"text":"\"How many rows are in my tables? Show them in a bar chart.\"","role":"string"},{"text":")","role":"function"}]]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Look at "},{"type":"code","text":"managed_agents"},{"type":"text","text":". You hand the workers to the supervisor in the same way that you handed tools to the agent in "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/agents","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"AI Agents"}]},{"type":"text","text":". This is because, from the supervisor's point of view, that is what the workers are: things it can call and get a result from."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"when-a-single-agent-might-be-better","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"When a Single Agent Might be Better"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Multi-agent is not an upgrade. It is a trade. You buy shorter prompts and a clearer separation between jobs. You pay for them in coordination and context transfer."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Stay with one agent while one prompt still fits comfortably and the tool list is still easy to manage. Split when a single system prompt tries to teach two unrelated jobs. When you split, split at the point where the least information has to cross from one agent to the other."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"summary","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Summary"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Several agents let each agent keep a short prompt and a single job. The supervisor architecture, where one agent delegates and collects, is the one to start with."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"You inherit two problems. The first is "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"coordination"}]},{"type":"text","text":": deciding who does what, and when it is done. The second is "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"context transfer"}]},{"type":"text","text":": deciding what each agent is handed. Those two are the whole difficulty. How you answer the second one (shared context or isolated context) shapes everything else."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"That is the core of Fundamentals. You now have the full picture:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"a model that only predicts text"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"a context window that is its desk"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"retrieval to fill that desk"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"tools to let it act"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"memory across turns"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"a loop that makes it an agent"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"several agents when one is not enough"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Everything in Intermediate is built on these seven ideas."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"references","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"References"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"-articles","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"📄 Articles"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/multi-agent"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Multi-agent systems"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the patterns, in more depth"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://langchain-ai.github.io/langgraph/concepts/multi_agent/"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"LangGraph multi-agent concepts"}]},{"type":"text","text":": supervisor, network and the rest, with code"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://www.langchain.com/blog/what-is-an-agent"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"What is an agent?"}]},{"type":"text","text":": worth reading again here, because a supervisor is a decision about autonomy"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"-modules","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"🧭 Modules"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/agents","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"AI Agents"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the single loop that all of this is built from"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"expert/advanced-multiagent","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Advanced Multi-Agent"}]},{"type":"text","text":": shared and isolated context, and agent-to-agent protocols"}]}]}]}],"draft":null,"quiz":[{"question":"According to the module, when should you split one agent into several agents?","options":["When the task needs more than one step to finish.","When the agent has to call more than one tool.","When one system prompt tries to teach two unrelated jobs.","Always, because several agents are an upgrade over one agent."],"answer":2,"explain":"Splitting then gives each agent a short prompt, a small tool list and one job. Multi-agent is a trade, not an upgrade."},{"question":"The module tells you to start with a supervisor. Why?","options":["Every decision passes through one place, so it is easier to debug.","Every agent can hand work to any other agent, so it is more flexible.","The workers decide among themselves who does each next step.","Workers need no handover, so context transfer is not a problem."],"answer":0,"explain":"In a supervisor design, every decision passes through one place, so you can see and debug what the system decided. A network is more flexible, but it is much harder to predict."},{"question":"Splitting the work across several agents creates two hard problems. Which two?","options":["Long system prompts and long tool lists","Wrong tool choices and confusion between two jobs","Tool calling and memory across turns","Coordination and context transfer between agents"],"answer":3,"explain":"Splitting the work creates both problems, and a single agent has neither. Long prompts and long tool lists are single-agent problems that splitting solves."},{"question":"What is the difference between shared context and isolated context?","options":["Shared: each agent sees only what it receives. Isolated: every agent sees everything.","Shared: every agent sees everything. Isolated: each agent sees only what it receives.","Shared: only the supervisor sees the context. Isolated: only the workers see it.","Shared: all agents use one model. Isolated: each agent uses its own model."],"answer":1,"explain":"With shared context, nothing gets lost in handover, but the context grows fast. With isolated context, each agent stays cheap and focused, but anything you forgot to pass is gone."}]}